          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong syllogism
            is a pattern of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deduction"} deductive
            argument of a certain limited range of form. The study of syllogisms was originated by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aristotle"} Aristotle
            and later developed by his followers as well as by the Stoics. Syllogisms were central objects of study for medieval logicians.
          %p
            Syllogisms involve two premises and a conclusion, each expressing a statement of the form that either all, none or some members of a class do or do not have a certain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/property"} property
          %ul
            %li No fish is a mammal.
            %li Some sea creatures are fish.
            %li Therefore, some sea creatures are not mammals.
          %p
            In the nineteenth century, syllogism became seen to be insufficiently expressive as more complicated relations and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantification"} quantification
            were encountered.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Predicate+logic"} Predicate logic
            came to take its place.
          %h2#original_definition Original definition
          %p
            The definition by Aristotle that has received the most attention (out of several variants) is from
            %em Prior Analytics
            24b18-22 (here taken from translation by
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Striker"} Striker (2009)
          %blockquote
            %p A ‘syllogismos’ is an argument (logos) in which, (i) certain things being posited (tethentôn), (ii) something other than what was laid down (keimenôn) (iii) results by necessity (eks anagkês sumbainei)(iv) because these things are so. By ‘because these things are so’ I mean that it results through these, and by ‘resulting through these’ I mean that no term is required from outside for the necessity to come about.
          %h2#related_entries Related entries
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/co-Heyting+negation"} co-Heyting negation
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Striker
            %li#SEP
              %p
                Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://plato.stanford.edu/entries/aristotle-logic/"} Aristotle’s logic
            %li
              %p
                Gisela Striker,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Aristotle’s Prior Analytics Book I: Translated with an Introduction and Commentary
                %a{:href => "http://philpapers.org/rec/STRAPA-2"} PhilPapers entry
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
                ,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy"} Lectures on the History of Philosophy
                    –
                    %a{:href => "Lectures+on+the+History+of+Philosophy#AristotleLogic"} Aristotle – Logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georg+Hegel"}> Georg Hegel
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
                  –
                  %a{:href => "Science+of+Logic#SchlussSyllogism"} Subjective logic – Subjectivity – Syllogism
          %p
            An interpretation of syllogistic term logic in modern
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            is discussed in detail in the textbook on
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+logic"} mathematical logic
          %ul
            %li#HilbertAckermann59
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Hilbert"}> David Hilbert
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilhelm+Ackermann"} Wilhelm Ackermann
              %em The calculus of classes (monadic predicate calculus)
              (
              %a{:href => "http://books.google.it/books?id=45ZGMjV9vfcC&pg=PA44#v=onepage&q&f=false"}> web
              ), chapter II of
              %em Grundzüge der Theoretischen Logik
              , 4th ed. Springer Heidelberg 1959 [1928]
          %p
            After J. Lukasiewicz’s classic monograph on Aristotelian logic from a modern point of view, the 1970s saw influential interpretations of it as systems of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+deduction"} natural deduction
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                J. Bacon,
                %em Natural-deduction rules for syllogistic
                , JSL
                %strong 31
                (1966) pp.686-7.
            %li
              %p
                J. Corcoran,
                %em Completeness of an ancient logic
                , JSL
                %strong 37
                (1972) pp.696-702.
            %li
              %p
                T. Smiley,
                %em What is a syllogism
                , JPL
                %strong 2
                (1973) pp.136-154.
          %p
            An approach to Aristotelian logic using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#PalmeReyesMacnamaraReyes94
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marie+La+Palme+Reyes"}> Marie La Palme Reyes
                , John Macnamara,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+Reyes"} Gonzalo Reyes
                ,
                %em Functoriality and Grammatical Role in Syllogisms
                , Notre Dame J. Formal Logic
                %strong 35
                no.1 (1994) pp.41-66. (
                %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/euclid.ndjfl/1040609293"}> Euclid
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://projecteuclid.org/download/pdf_1/euclid.ndjfl/1040609293"} pdf
            %li#RRZ94
              %p
                M. La Palme Reyes, J. Macnamara,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gonzalo+E.+Reyes"} G. E. Reyes
                %em The non-Boolean logic of natural language negation
                , Phil. Math.
                %strong 2
                no.1 (1994) pp.45-68.
          %p
            A proposal for formalization of syllogisms within
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+logic"} linear logic
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Ruggero Pagnan,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A diagrammatic calculus of syllogisms
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1001.1707"} arXiv:1001.1707
            %li#Pagnan13
              %p
                Ruggero Pagnan,
                %em Syllogisms in Rudimentary Linear Logic, Diagrammatically
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1302.7111"}> arXiv:1302.7111
                )
        .revisedby
